.\" sball-integration manual page
.TH SBALL-INTEGRATION 8 "2026-10-14" "sball-addition 1.0.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
sball-integration \- wire (or unwire) the sball-addition hook wrapper into the sballd service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sball-integration
.RI [ status | apply | remove ]
.RI [ \-\-mode= dropin | shim ]
.RI [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RI [ \-\-yes ]
.RI [ \-\-root= dir ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The rule this command exists for:
.B sball-addition never touches
.BR sballd.service .
No installer, package, rollout, unit or daemon of this project starts, stops,
restarts, enables, disables or reconfigures the PVM service, and none of them
writes a unit file, drop-in or binary that belongs to it.
.PP
Hooks therefore do not run at start until an administrator decides that they
should, and that decision is made with
.BR sball-integration .
It is the only component of the project that writes a file belonging to the
service, and it is only ever run by hand. It writes the file and prints the
.BR systemctl (1)
command
.B you
have to run; it does not call
.B systemctl
itself.
.PP
Until it is applied, the rest of the project works read-only: it polls the PVM
socket, reports state, exports metrics and runs hooks on demand with
.BR "sball-addition run\-phase" .
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B status
Report whether the drop-in or the shim is applied, and what an administrator
would have to do next. Exit status 0 when something is applied, 1 when nothing
is.
.TP
.B apply
Write the integration (see
.BR \-\-mode ).
The file is written atomically and refuses to overwrite a foreign file without
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
.B remove
Undo it: delete the drop-in, or restore the real daemon and delete the wrapper
copy. The change takes effect at the next start of the daemon \(em reloading and
restarting stays with you.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-mode=dropin
Default. Write
.I /etc/systemd/system/sballd.service.d/sball-addition.conf
with an empty
.B ExecStart
followed by
.BR "ExecStart=/usr/bin/sballd-wrapper" .
The drop-in leaves the vendor unit file untouched, survives PVM updates and is
reverted by deleting one file.
.TP
.B \-\-mode=shim
Move
.I /usr/sbin/sballd
to
.I /usr/sbin/sballd.real
and install the wrapper as
.I /usr/sbin/sballd
instead. Use this when starts bypass systemd (for example
.BR "pvm-wrapper \-\-start" ).
It is a larger change: the wrapper is now in the path of every start.
.TP
.B \-\-print
Print the drop-in on stdout and change nothing. Useful for configuration
management that distributes the file itself.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would change, including the file content, and execute nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Skip safety prompts: overwrite a differing drop-in, or apply the shim without
the confirmation.
.TP
.BI \-\-root= dir
Treat
.I dir
as the filesystem root (tests, chroots, recovery environments).
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix= dir
Prefix for the
.I /usr
paths (defaults to the root).
.SH AFTER APPLYING
The hooks run the next time the daemon starts, and only then. Sequence that keeps
a node safe:
.PP
.RS 4
sball-addition validate
.br
sball-addition run-phase pre-quorate \-\-dry-run
.br
sball-node-verify \-\-smoke
.br
sball-integration apply
.br
systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart sballd.service   \fI(your call, your moment)\fR
.br
sball-addition watcher \-\-once && sball-addition status
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success; for
.B status
it means the integration is applied
.TP
.B 1
.B status
only: nothing is applied
.TP
.B 2
usage error, or a refusal (a differing file without
.BR \-\-yes ,
no wrapper installed)
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/sballd.service.d/sball-addition.conf
The drop-in written by
.BR "apply \-\-mode=dropin" .
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/sballd.real
The preserved daemon when
.B \-\-mode=shim
was used.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sball-addition (8),
.BR sballd-wrapper (8),
.BR sball-node-verify (8),
.BR sball-rollout (8),
.BR systemctl (1)
